2728,"R","Bonarda Piemontese"],["Barbera",2453,"R","Barbera"],["Verdicchio Bianco",1951,"W","Verdicchio Bianco"],["Other",15379,"","other"]],"legal":["no","main","main","main","main","main","main","main","main","no","main","main",""]},"terrain":{"elev":[60,110,670],"slope":7.8,"flat":0.51,"steep":0.28,"zoom":11,"tiles":16,"cells":338150},"area_km2":2385,"rocks":{"families":[["limestone",0.43],["gravel",0.36],["sand",0.07],["clay",0.07],["evaporite",0.07]],"points":7,"scale":"medium","names":{"limestone":"Limestone","gravel":"Gravel","sand":"Sand","clay":"Clay","evaporite":"Gypsum and salt"}},"large":false,"reading":{"climate":[["Grapes","Seasons this warm ripen Chardonnay, Merlot, Sauvignon Blanc, Sémillon and Cabernet Franc fully without cooking off their freshness."],["Grown here","Chardonnay and Merlot sit in their sweet spot, so the wines can show the style they are known for."],["Grown here","Pinot Gris is at its warm limit. It can ripen too fast, giving heavy, soft wine that has lost its freshness."],["Grown here","Pinot Noir is at its warm limit. It can ripen too fast, giving heavy, soft wine that has lost its freshness."],["Harvest","Rain picks up at harvest. Growers race to pick before rot sets in, and vintages vary more."]],"soil":{"rock":"Limestone makes alkaline soil that drains well yet can hold water for the vines. It is usually planted to grapes valued for their acidity. Gravel drains fast and is poor, so roots go deep for water and food. It holds little water.","top":"The topsoil holds water well and drains at a steady pace, so vines rarely go thirsty or sit in wet ground. Its pH is close to neutral, so vines take up the nutrients they need easily."},"land":[["Height","The high ground runs about 7°F cooler than the low ground. Higher vineyards ripen later and keep more acidity, so one grape can make lighter or richer wine here depending on height."],["Slope","Much of it is steep. Cold air and rain run off, so frost and waterlogging are rare and soils stay thin, keeping yields low. Steep rows are slow and costly to work, often by hand."]]},"winkler":["II","Early and mid-season grapes make good wine."],"sources":[{"name":"Wikidata sitelinks and Wikimedia page views","url":"https://www.wikidata.org"},{"name":"Candiago et al. 2022, A geospatial inventory of regulatory information for wine protected designations of origin in Europe, Scientific Data (product specifications as of 2021)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-022-01513-0"},{"name":"Fick and Hijmans 2017, WorldClim 2.1 monthly averages for 1970 to 2000, 2.5 minute grid (about 4.5 km)","url":"https://www.worldclim.org/data/worldclim21.html"},{"name":"Beck et al. 2023, High-resolution (1 km) Koppen-Geiger maps, Scientific Data (1991 to 2020)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-02549-6"},{"name":"Winkler, Cook, Kliewer and Lider 1974, General Viticulture, University of California Press (heat summation regions)","url":null},{"name":"Wikipedia, Winkler index (what each region suits)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Winkler_index&oldid=1379995815"},{"name":"Jones 2010, Climate, grapes, and wine: structure and suitability in a variable and changing climate, VIII International Terroir Congress (which grapes suit which growing season temperatures)","url":"https://ives-openscience.eu/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/Climate_Grape_Wine_Jones.pdf"},{"name":"ISRIC SoilGrids 2.0, 1 km aggregates, 30 to 60 cm depth (CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://www.isric.org/explore/soilgrids"},{"name":"USDA NRCS Soil Survey Manual, Agriculture Handbook 18 (texture classes and pH classes)","url":null},{"name":"Anderson, Nelgen and Puga, Database of Regional, National and Global Winegrape Bearing Areas by Variety, 2000 to 2023, University of Adelaide (CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://doi.org/10.25909/32870405.v1"},{"name":"AWS Terrain Tiles (Terrarium), from SRTM, GMTED2010, ETOPO1 and national elevation models","url":"https://registry.opendata.aws/terrain-tiles/"},{"name":"W